From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000017 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sf2000-linux <sf2000-linux@example.invalid>
Date: Mon, 11 May 2026 11:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mips: sf2000: skip early Wired read

Physical SF2000 hardware now reaches the first local_flush_tlb_all() pass and
stops immediately after EntryLo0/EntryLo1 are cleared. The next instruction in
the generated kernel is the CP0 Wired read used by num_wired_entries().

For the SF2000 bring-up profile this flush is called from r4k_tlb_configure()
immediately after Linux writes CP0 Wired to zero. There are no Linux-owned
wired entries yet, and the bring-up profile does not install platform wired
TLB mappings. Avoid rereading CP0 Wired on this early path and use the known
zero value so the kernel can proceed to the first indexed TLB write.
---
 arch/mips/mm/tlb-r4k.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/mips/mm/tlb-r4k.c b/arch/mips/mm/tlb-r4k.c
index 6c6f997bdc4b..72e874d6af54 100644
--- a/arch/mips/mm/tlb-r4k.c
+++ b/arch/mips/mm/tlb-r4k.c
@@ -74,7 +74,13 @@ void local_flush_tlb_all(void)
 	write_c0_entrylo1(0);
 	sf2000_tlb_tick("tlb-flush-after-entrylo-clear");
 
-	entry = num_wired_entries();
+	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_MIPS_SF2000)) {
+		/* r4k_tlb_configure() has just written CP0 Wired to zero. */
+		entry = 0;
+		sf2000_tlb_tick("tlb-flush-after-wired-skip");
+	} else {
+		entry = num_wired_entries();
+	}
 	first_entry = entry;
 	sf2000_tlb_tick("tlb-flush-after-wired-read");
 
-- 
2.50.1
